// @corpus: fixture
// @id: OWNED-Notes
// @category: application data — an owned table: whose a row is, who else reads it, what they see (ADR-0384 H1)
// @charter: §7.8, §9.2
// @milestone: E14
//
// Track `row-ownership` (ADR-0384's H1): a table that states whose its rows
// are. A note is its author's: every read of a note leads with the reader's
// handle in a field the note names its readers by, which only the platform
// makes (ADR-0263), so no program reads or writes another's notes by naming
// an id, and the host holds each handle to the reader it serves. A note is
// read by the one it is addressed to as well, without its draft: they are
// answered `NoteSeen`, the record the compiler declares beside `Note` with no
// `draft` in it, so no page of theirs can hold one.
//
// What it gives: `Notes.get(author, id)`, `Notes.get_many(author, ids)`,
// `Notes.of_author(author)`, `Notes.by_to(to) -> List<NoteSeen>`, and the
// writes `Notes.insert(author, note)`, `Notes.put(author, note)`,
// `Notes.delete(author, id)` and `Notes.delete_of_author(author)`, each
// `author` and `to` a `User<UserId>`, the reader's handle. No `all`, and no
// read by any other field.

module Notes

import capability.{ UserId }

opaque type NoteId = String

// What a note says: at most a few lines.
opaque type Text = String where String.length(value) <= 280

type Note = Note { author: UserId, id: NoteId, to: UserId, text: Text, draft: Text, added: Int }

table Notes
    row     Note
    key     author, id
    order   added
    index   by_to on to
    owned   by author
    shares  with to
    private draft
